ONEDRAFT
CAD-AI — Aria Powered
Identity, Access & Security System
Version 1.0
Status: Implementation Specification
Security Model: Identity + Organization + Project + Capability
Authentication: Bearer Token / OAuth 2.0 Compatible
Authorization: Role-Based + Scope-Based + Resource-Based
Audit: Immutable Security Events
Primary Boundary: Organization → Project → Resource
AI Layer: Aria Powered

1. SECURITY DESIGN PRINCIPLE
OneDraft shall treat security as an architectural subsystem rather than an application feature.
Every operation entering the platform must establish:
Who is making the request
Which organization the request belongs to
Which project is being accessed
Which resource is being accessed
Which capability is being requested
Whether the requested operation is authorized
Whether the operation must be audited
The security subsystem therefore becomes the authorization boundary between external actors and the OneDraft computational project model.

2. SECURITY ARCHITECTURE
The security architecture shall operate through the following chain:
CLIENT
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
AUTHENTICATION
 ↓
SESSION / TOKEN
 ↓
ORGANIZATION MEMBERSHIP
 ↓
PROJECT ACCESS
 ↓
RESOURCE AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
CAPABILITY AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
DOMAIN SERVICE
 ↓
EVENT
 ↓
AUDIT
No external client may bypass this boundary to directly access project state.

3. SECURITY BOUNDARIES
OneDraft shall establish the following security boundaries:
INTERNET
    ↓
EDGE / API GATEWAY
    ↓
AUTHENTICATION
    ↓
APPLICATION API
    ↓
DOMAIN SERVICES
    ↓
WORKERS
    ↓
DATABASE / OBJECT STORAGE
Internal services shall authenticate to one another.
A network location alone shall not constitute authorization.

4. SECURITY PRINCIPLES
The implementation shall follow:
Least privilege
Explicit authorization
Deny by default
Resource isolation
Tenant isolation
Credential minimization
Immutable auditing
Short-lived credentials
Separation of duties
Defense in depth
Re-authentication for sensitive operations

5. IDENTITY DOMAIN
The identity subsystem shall manage:
Users
Organizations
Memberships
Roles
Credentials
Sessions
Service identities
API clients
Security events
The existing identity schema remains the authoritative source for application-level identity.

6. USER IDENTITY
The existing:
identity.users
table remains the primary application identity record.
The user identity shall not contain authentication secrets directly unless explicitly required by the selected identity implementation.
Authentication credentials should remain separated from project-domain records.

7. USER STATUS
Initial user statuses:
INVITED
ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
DISABLED
DELETED
Only:
ACTIVE
users may authenticate normally.
Suspended or disabled identities must fail authorization even where a previously issued credential has not yet expired.

8. ORGANIZATION SECURITY BOUNDARY
The organization is the primary tenant boundary.
Organization
    ↓
Members
    ↓
Projects
    ↓
Project Resources
A user must never obtain access to another organization's project merely because the resource identifier is known.

9. PROJECT SECURITY BOUNDARY
Every project request shall establish:
organization_id
project_id
user_id
The API shall verify that:
user
 ↓
member of organization
 ↓
authorized for project
before executing the requested domain operation.

10. MEMBERSHIP ROLES
Initial organization roles:
OWNER
ADMIN
MEMBER
VIEWER
BILLING
Project-specific roles may additionally include:
PROJECT_ADMIN
PROJECT_EDITOR
PROJECT_REVIEWER
PROJECT_VIEWER
Roles are permissions containers and shall not replace explicit capability checks.

11. CAPABILITY MODEL
The platform shall use capability scopes such as:
project:read
project:write

model:read
model:write

aria:execute
aria:approve

geometry:read
geometry:generate

drawing:read
drawing:generate

redline:read
redline:write

compliance:read
compliance:execute

validation:read
validation:execute

document:read
document:generate

acceptance:write

event:read

admin:organization
admin:project
admin:security

12. AUTHORIZATION MODEL
Authorization shall evaluate three dimensions:
IDENTITY
+
RESOURCE
+
CAPABILITY
Conceptually:
Can User X
perform Capability Y
against Resource Z?
A valid authentication token alone is insufficient.

13. AUTHORIZATION DECISION
Authorization decisions shall resolve to:
ALLOW
DENY
REAUTH_REQUIRED
The application shall not infer authorization from client-side state.
The server remains authoritative.

14. AUTHENTICATION
The MVP shall use bearer-token authentication compatible with:
Authorization: Bearer <token>
The authentication layer shall validate:
issuer
audience
signature
expiration
issued-at
subject
scopes
token status

15. TOKEN PRINCIPLE
Tokens shall contain the minimum information required to establish identity and authorization context.
Sensitive project information shall not be embedded into bearer tokens unnecessarily.
Project authorization remains a server-side decision.

16. TOKEN EXPIRATION
Access tokens shall be short-lived.
Long-lived credentials shall not be used as ordinary access tokens.
Refresh mechanisms, where implemented, shall use protected refresh credentials and revocation controls.

17. SESSION MANAGEMENT
Authenticated sessions shall support:
session_id
user_id
issued_at
expires_at
last_activity
client_metadata
status
revoked_at
Session statuses:
ACTIVE
EXPIRED
REVOKED

18. SESSION REVOCATION
Security administrators must be able to revoke active sessions.
Revocation events shall be recorded.
A revoked session must no longer authorize API access.

19. MULTI-FACTOR AUTHENTICATION
The architecture shall support MFA without requiring the domain model to change.
Sensitive operations may require elevated authentication.
Potential sensitive operations include:
organization administration
security administration
credential changes
service-account changes
billing administration
acceptance authorization
production configuration

20. REAUTHENTICATION
Operations designated as high assurance may require:
recent authentication
+
required capability
This prevents a long-lived authenticated browser session from automatically authorizing high-impact operations.

21. SERVICE IDENTITIES
Internal workers and services shall not authenticate as ordinary users.
The system shall support service identities:
service_id
name
status
scopes
created_at
updated_at
Examples:
geometry-worker
validation-worker
documentation-worker
job-worker
event-consumer
artifact-service

22. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE AUTHENTICATION
Internal services shall authenticate through controlled service credentials.
The architecture becomes:
SERVICE A
 ↓
SERVICE IDENTITY
 ↓
AUTHORIZED CAPABILITY
 ↓
SERVICE B
A worker shall receive only the permissions necessary to perform its assigned function.

23. WORKER SECURITY
Workers shall not receive unrestricted database credentials.
The preferred architecture is:
JOB
 ↓
WORKER
 ↓
DOMAIN SERVICE
 ↓
AUTHORIZED OPERATION
Workers may consume jobs while domain services retain control over state mutation.

24. ARIA SECURITY BOUNDARY
Aria shall operate entirely inside the OneDraft capability model.
Aria shall not receive unrestricted credentials to:
PostgreSQL
Object Storage
Identity Provider
Billing
Production Infrastructure
Aria requests capabilities.
The application decides whether those capabilities may execute.

25. ARIA AUTHORIZATION
An Aria command shall establish:
user_id
organization_id
project_id
revision_id
command_id
requested_capability
The command boundary shall then evaluate:
identity authorization
+
project authorization
+
command authorization
+
model constraints
+
revision state

26. ARIA APPROVAL BOUNDARY
Operations may be divided into:
READ
PROPOSE
EXECUTE
APPROVE
PUBLISH
This allows OneDraft to distinguish between:
Aria proposing a change
and:
Aria executing an authorized change

27. PRIVILEGED OPERATIONS
Privileged operations shall be explicitly enumerated.
Examples:
DELETE_PROJECT
CHANGE_ORGANIZATION_OWNER
CHANGE_SECURITY_POLICY
CREATE_SERVICE_IDENTITY
ROTATE_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL
MODIFY_PRODUCTION_CONFIGURATION
PUBLISH_FINAL_PACKAGE
RECORD_ACCEPTANCE
No privileged operation shall be activated merely because a user is authenticated.

28. RESOURCE AUTHORIZATION
Authorization shall be applied recursively.
For example:
Project
 ↓
Building
 ↓
Level
 ↓
Space
 ↓
Element
 ↓
Geometry
Access to an element derives from access to the containing project unless an explicit resource policy states otherwise.

29. ARTIFACT AUTHORIZATION
Stored artifacts shall inherit project authorization.
An artifact reference shall therefore resolve through:
artifact
 ↓
project
 ↓
organization
 ↓
authorized principal
Direct knowledge of an object-storage key shall not grant access.

30. DOCUMENT AUTHORIZATION
Generated document packages shall be protected by the same project boundary.
A document package shall identify:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
validation_run_id
Access requires authorization to the associated project.

31. EVENT AUTHORIZATION
Events may contain sensitive project information.
Event consumers shall therefore receive only events appropriate to their identity and scope.
External clients shall not automatically receive internal event-bus traffic.

32. EVENT SECURITY
Security-sensitive events shall include:
actor_id
organization_id
project_id
event_type
timestamp
request_id
correlation_id
source
Where appropriate:
ip_hash
client_id
service_id
may also be recorded.

