37. IDEMPOTENCY SECURITY
Mutating requests shall continue to support:
Idempotency-Key
The security subsystem shall bind idempotency records to the authenticated principal and appropriate resource context.
This prevents one identity from replaying another identity's request context.

38. API GATEWAY SECURITY
The API gateway shall perform preliminary:
TLS termination
token validation
request size limits
rate limiting
request identification
basic threat filtering
Domain authorization remains inside application services.

39. RATE LIMITING
Rate limits shall be applied by appropriate dimensions:
IP
user
organization
service identity
API endpoint
operation type
High-impact operations may have lower limits than read operations.

40. ARIA RATE LIMITING
Aria operations shall have explicit execution controls.
Limits may apply to:
commands per minute
concurrent commands
geometry jobs
document generation jobs
validation jobs
The objective is to prevent uncontrolled automated mutation.

41. INPUT VALIDATION
All external inputs shall be validated before reaching domain services.
Validation includes:
type
format
length
range
enumeration
resource ownership
authorization
schema
Client-side validation is supplementary only.

42. COMMAND VALIDATION
An incoming command shall pass through:
authentication
 ↓
authorization
 ↓
schema validation
 ↓
project state validation
 ↓
constraint validation
 ↓
domain execution
A malformed or unauthorized command shall never reach the mutation boundary.

43. SECRET MANAGEMENT
Secrets shall not be stored in:
source code
Git repositories
OpenAPI documents
database seed data
frontend bundles
logs
event payloads
Secrets shall be supplied through controlled secret-management infrastructure.

44. ENVIRONMENT SEPARATION
OneDraft shall maintain separate environments:
DEVELOPMENT
TEST
STAGING
PRODUCTION
Credentials and signing keys must not be shared between environments.

45. PRODUCTION ACCESS
Production administrative access shall be restricted.
Production access should require:
authenticated identity
+
explicit role
+
appropriate capability
+
audit event

46. DATABASE SECURITY
Application services shall use restricted database roles.
Conceptually:
READ SERVICE
WRITE SERVICE
MIGRATION SERVICE
ADMIN SERVICE
Database credentials shall not be embedded in frontend applications.

47. ROW-LEVEL ISOLATION
Where appropriate, PostgreSQL Row-Level Security may reinforce organization/project isolation.
The primary authorization decision remains in the application layer.
Defense-in-depth may therefore become:
APPLICATION AUTHORIZATION
+
DATABASE ISOLATION

48. OBJECT STORAGE SECURITY
Object storage shall use private buckets/containers by default.
Artifacts shall be retrieved through authorized application mechanisms.
Temporary signed URLs may be generated for controlled downloads.
Signed URLs shall have limited lifetimes.

49. ENCRYPTION
Data shall be protected:
in transit
through modern TLS.
Sensitive stored information shall use appropriate:
encryption at rest
provided by the selected infrastructure.

50. PASSWORD SECURITY
Where OneDraft directly manages passwords, passwords shall never be stored in plaintext.
They shall be processed through a modern password hashing mechanism.
However, the preferred architecture is to delegate authentication to a dedicated identity provider where practical.

51. API CLIENT CREDENTIALS
External integrations may receive API credentials scoped to:
organization
project
capability
Credentials shall support:
creation
rotation
expiration
revocation
audit

52. WEBHOOK SECURITY
Outbound webhooks shall use signed payloads.
Each webhook shall provide:
event_id
timestamp
signature
payload
Consumers shall verify the signature before accepting the event.

53. EVENT REPLAY PROTECTION
Event consumers shall protect against duplicate and replayed events.
Each event shall have a unique:
event_id
Consumers shall maintain sufficient processing state to safely recognize previously processed events.

54. SECURITY POLICY
The system shall support organization-level security policy configuration.
Initial policy categories:
session_policy
authentication_policy
mfa_policy
api_policy
artifact_policy
retention_policy
integration_policy
Policy changes shall be audited.

55. SECURITY POLICY HIERARCHY
Policy resolution shall follow:
PLATFORM POLICY
 ↓
ORGANIZATION POLICY
 ↓
PROJECT POLICY
 ↓
RESOURCE POLICY
A lower-level policy may not weaken a mandatory platform security requirement.

56. DATA ACCESS LOGGING
Sensitive reads may require audit logging in addition to mutating operations.
Examples:
document download
project export
security configuration access
customer acceptance record access
professional review access

57. EXPORT SECURITY
Project exports shall require authorization.
Exports shall preserve:
project identity
revision identity
model version
document provenance
The export operation shall generate an audit event.

58. DELETION SECURITY
Deletion shall be treated as a privileged state transition.
For project-level objects:
ACTIVE
 ↓
DELETED
Normal application operation shall prefer soft deletion.
Historical records remain protected from ordinary deletion.

59. RETENTION
Retention policies shall distinguish:
active project state
historical revisions
audit events
security events
generated documents
temporary artifacts
job records
The platform shall not use one universal deletion policy for all classes of data.

60. SECURITY INCIDENT STATE
The architecture shall support security incident markers without altering project history.
An incident may reference:
organization
project
user
service
request
event
artifact
job
command
This permits security investigations without corrupting the underlying project model.

61. COMPROMISED CREDENTIAL RESPONSE
Credentials shall be capable of immediate revocation.
The response sequence becomes:
DETECT
 ↓
REVOKE
 ↓
INVALIDATE SESSIONS
 ↓
ROTATE CREDENTIALS
 ↓
AUDIT
 ↓
REVIEW
The project model itself remains unchanged unless a separately authorized domain operation occurs.

62. SECURITY AND REVISION INTEGRITY
Security operations must not silently create architectural model revisions.
A security event and a model revision are distinct concepts.
SECURITY EVENT ≠ MODEL REVISION
This preserves the separation between:
what happened to the system
and:
what changed in the architectural project model

63. SECURITY AND COMPLIANCE
The security subsystem shall integrate with the existing compliance and validation architecture without replacing it.
For example:
SECURITY POLICY
 ↓
AUTHORIZED OPERATION
 ↓
DOMAIN COMMAND
 ↓
COMPLIANCE VALIDATION
 ↓
MODEL REVISION
Security authorization occurs before domain execution.
Regulatory validation evaluates the resulting project state.

64. SECURITY AND ACCEPTANCE
Customer acceptance remains a domain operation.
The security subsystem determines whether the authenticated principal has the capability to submit acceptance.
The acceptance record remains part of the established revision/document provenance chain.

